Cell[TextData[{
 "OK.\n\nAt the top of this file, I wrote that we only \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]start\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] with the three-body \
problem here. By this I mean at least two things: \[LineSeparator]1. The \
above is ",
 StyleBox["not general ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "(or even close). It\[CloseCurlyQuote]s very far from describing ",
 StyleBox["all",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " aspects of any of the three particles. Even for two particles, in basic \
courses we rarely attempt to describe ",
 StyleBox["all",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " aspects of e.g. actual electrons simultaneously. What is done for example \
in the Intro Mod.Phys. course is to a) motivate the introduction of the basic \
object, the qubit, using the electron\[CloseCurlyQuote]s spin, b) switch to \
talking about the qubit as ",
 StyleBox["any two-state system",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " abstractly. For example, maybe a more basic question about whether a \
particle has spin is whether it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s present at all: we can use \
the qubit to distinguish this, e.g. spin up = present, spin down = not \
present. (This is the ",
 StyleBox["Jordan-Wigner transformation",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", which I discuss elsewhere.)\[LineSeparator]2. The above is ",
 StyleBox["not final",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " (or even close). By this I mean that teaching of basic quantum physics is \
evolving rapidly as we give these courses, with more about entanglement \
moving to earlier courses. There is no universally-agreed upon way to \
characterize entanglement for many-body systems. For two, entanglement \
entropy is enough, but already for three particles, nontrivial choices \
appear. For example, now the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]environment\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] of any particle consists of ",
 StyleBox["two",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " other particles, so should we take into account whether ",
 StyleBox["they",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " are entangled among each other? \n",
 StyleBox["Summary",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ": the above is probably not how we will express these things in a few years \
from now. In fact in my opinion, no universally useful and complete way to \
describe three quantum particles is known, especially when interactions are \
taken into account. This should maybe not be so surprising, given the \
difficulty of the classical three-body problem! "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.976956791840972*^9, 3.976957198950965*^9}, {
  3.976957801499422*^9, 3.9769579377060204`*^9}, {3.98178988946069*^9, 
  3.981789899792622*^9}, {3.9817931987024927`*^9, 
  3.981793199535203*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a6974ad1-f1d8-4aac-bbdf-\
18c9826d2bc9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "[I have another Mathematica file on ",
 StyleBox["non",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "-constant Yang-Baxter. I\[CloseCurlyQuote]m not sure it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
useful yet.]"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.976852038811131*^9, 3.97685204592134*^9}, {
  3.97689441986543*^9, 3.9768944338619137`*^9}, {3.976956718022664*^9, 
